      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt} + 4 y = - 2 y^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following are equilibrium solutions? Select all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An equilibrium is a constant function $y(t)=c$. Set $dy/dt=0$ and test the resulting equation.\n\nRearranging once, test whether $f(c)=0$, where\n\n$$\nf(c) = - 2 c^{2} - 4 c.\n$$\n\n- For $c=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) &=-8 + 8 \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is zero, so it is an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) &=-4 - 2 \\\\ &=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) &=0 + 0 \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is zero, so it is an equilibrium solution.\n\nSelect $y=-2$ and $y=0$.",
